The '''Internet Party''' was a registered [[political party]] in [[New Zealand]] that promoted [[Internet freedom]] and [[Internet privacy|privacy]]. The party was founded in January 2014 with the financial support and promotion of internet entrepreneur [[Kim Dotcom]], and was first led by former [[Alliance (New Zealand political party)|Alliance]] MP [[Laila Harré]], then by citizen journalist Suzie Dawson.

The party contested the [[2014 New Zealand general election|2014 New Zealand election]] as part of an electoral alliance with the [[Mana Movement]]. It also contested the [[2017 New Zealand general election|2017 general election]], independent of Mana. In both cases it did not win any seats in the [[New Zealand House of Representatives]].<ref name="2014 general election results2">{{cite web|title=New Zealand 2014 General Election Official Results|url=http://www.elections.org.nz/news-media/new-zealand-2014-general-election-official-results|url-status=dead|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20180613134004/http://www.elections.org.nz/news-media/new-zealand-2014-general-election-official-results|archive-date=13 June 2018|access-date=7 October 2017|publisher=[[Electoral Commission (New Zealand)|New Zealand Electoral Commission]]}}</ref><ref name="2017 general election results2">{{cite web|title=2017 General Election - Official Result|url=http://www.electionresults.govt.nz/electionresults_2017/|access-date=24 September 2017|publisher=[[Electoral Commission (New Zealand)|New Zealand Electoral Commission]]}}</ref> The party was deregistered by the [[Electoral Commission (New Zealand)|New Zealand Electoral Commission]] in June 2018 after its membership dropped below the 500 required for registration.<ref name="CANCELLATION OF PARTY REGISTRATION2">{{cite news|title=Cancellation of Party Registration|publisher=New Zealand Electoral Commission|url=http://www.elections.org.nz/news-media/cancellation-party-registration-0|url-status=dead|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20180614021243/http://www.elections.org.nz/news-media/cancellation-party-registration-0|archive-date=14 June 2018}}</ref>

The party has not contested local or general elections since the 2017 general election. The party applied for broadcasting funding for the [[2020 New Zealand general election|2020 general election]], but did not contest the election.

==History==
[[Kim Dotcom]] founded the file-sharing website [[Megaupload]] in 2005. It was shut down in January 2012 by the US government and Dotcom was arrested by the New Zealand Police. In September 2013, Dotcom revealed an interest in setting up a political party<ref>{{cite news|date=1 September 2013|title=Kim Dotcom to enter politics?|publisher=[[Fairfax New Zealand]]|url=http://www.stuff.co.nz/national/politics/9113113/Kim-Dotcom-to-enter-politics|url-status=dead|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20131109044456/http://www.stuff.co.nz/national/politics/9113113/Kim-Dotcom-to-enter-politics|archive-date=9 November 2013}}</ref> and on 15 January 2014, Dotcom announced the creation of the Internet Party, including its name and logo.<ref name="nzh12">{{cite news|author=Patrice Dougan|date=15 January 2014|title=Kim Dotcom reveals name of new political party|newspaper=[[New Zealand Herald]] |url=http://www.nzherald.co.nz/politics/news/article.cfm?c_id=280&objectid=11186820|access-date=15 January 2014}}</ref><ref>{{cite news|date=15 January 2014|title=Kim Dotcom unveils the Internet Party|publisher=3 News|url=http://www.3news.co.nz/Kim-Dotcom-unveils-the-Internet-Party/tabid/1607/articleID/328494/Default.aspx|access-date=15 January 2014|archive-date=16 January 2014|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20140116074235/http://www.3news.co.nz/Kim-Dotcom-unveils-the-Internet-Party/tabid/1607/articleID/328494/Default.aspx|url-status=dead}}</ref> He intended to hold a launch party on 20 January, two years after the raid on his house and the day before his 40th birthday. He distributed 25,000 tickets but was forced to cancel for fear of breaching electoral law.<ref name="nzh2">{{cite news|last=Milne|first=Jonathan|date=23 March 2014|title=Politicians of all stripes welcome at Kim's place|newspaper=[[The New Zealand Herald]] |url=http://www.nzherald.co.nz/politics/news/article.cfm?c_id=280&objectid=11224539|access-date=23 March 2014}}</ref><ref>{{cite news|last=Davison|first=Isaac|date=16 January 2014|title=Kim Dotcom cans Auckland party|newspaper=[[The New Zealand Herald]] |url=http://www.nzherald.co.nz/nz/news/article.cfm?c_id=1&objectid=11187592|access-date=23 March 2014}}</ref>

The Internet Party started to sign up members on 27 March 2014,<ref>{{cite news|last=Bennett|first=Adam|date=27 March 2014|title=Kim Dotcom launches Internet Party|newspaper=[[The New Zealand Herald]] |url=http://www.nzherald.co.nz/nz/news/article.cfm?c_id=1&objectid=11227530|access-date=27 March 2014}}</ref> and was the first New Zealand party to do so through the use of a phone app.<ref>{{cite news|last=Bennett|first=Adam|date=24 March 2014|title=Dotcom's Internet Party app approved|newspaper=[[The New Zealand Herald]] |url=http://www.nzherald.co.nz/nz/news/article.cfm?c_id=1&objectid=11225407|access-date=24 March 2014}}</ref> It became a registered political party on 13 May 2014.<ref>{{cite web|date=13 May 2014|title=Registration of Internet Party and Logo|url=http://www.elections.org.nz/news-media/registration-internet-party-and-logo|access-date=13 May 2014|publisher=New Zealand Electoral Commission}}</ref> Dotcom provided NZ$3.5 million in funding to the party, which was the largest personal contribution to a political party on record in New Zealand.<ref>{{cite news|last=Hutchison|first=Jonathan|date=18 September 2014|title=Online Renegade, Wanted in U.S., Shakes Up New Zealand Election|newspaper=[[The New Zealand Herald]] |url=https://www.nytimes.com/2014/09/19/world/asia/kim-dotcom-online-renegade-shakes-up-new-zealand-election.html|access-date=7 October 2017}}</ref> To select its first leader, the party ran an [[Idols (franchise)|Idol]]-style candidate search and appointed [[Laila Harré]], a former Alliance MP and cabinet minister.

===2014 election===
{{See also|Internet Party and Mana Movement}}In May 2014, Internet Party chief executive Vikram Kumar and Mana Movement leader [[Hone Harawira]] announced a merger of the two parties, to be known as the [[Internet Party and Mana Movement]], or the abbreviated Internet Mana.<ref name="MOU">{{cite web|date=25 May 2014|title=Memorandum of Understanding between the MANA Movement and the Internet Party|url=https://docs.google.com/file/d/0B-OsCSmT5K89LUwxOExmUjJpN2c/edit|access-date=10 August 2014}}</ref> The party and its logo were registered with the [[Electoral Commission (New Zealand)|New Zealand Electoral Commission]] on 24 July 2014, allowing the coalition party to contest the party vote.<ref name="Internet MANA registration">{{cite web|date=24 July 2014|title=Registration of Internet Party and MANA Movement logo|url=http://www.elections.org.nz/news-media/registration-internet-party-and-mana-movement-logo|access-date=8 August 2014|publisher=Electoral Commission}}</ref> The Internet Party and Mana Movement contested the [[New Zealand general election, 2014|2014 general election]] as a single entity. Mana already held an electoral seat in Parliament – Harawira held the [[Māori electorates|Māori electorate]] of [[Te Tai Tokerau]] – and expected to retain this seat. Under New Zealand's electoral rules, a party without an electoral seat needed 5% of the party vote to enter Parliament, but if it won an electorate seat it would be entitled to enter Parliament and potentially bring in more MPs. The memorandum of understanding stated that the agreement would remain in force until at least six weeks after polling day. The two component parties agreed to review their arrangement within five weeks of the election.<ref name="MOU" />

Multiple [[Opinion polling for the 2014 New Zealand general election|opinion polls prior to the election]] suggested Internet Mana could receive more than two per cent of the party vote,<ref>{{Cite web|title=National (48%) down but still holds clear election winning lead over Labour/ Greens (40%) as Prime Minister John Key announces $212 million 'roading and construction' package for regional New Zealand|url=http://www.roymorgan.com/findings/5664-new-zealand-roy-morgan-poll-july-2-2014-201407020558|access-date=31 January 2021|website=Roy Morgan|language=en|archive-date=7 July 2014|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20140707050919/http://www.roymorgan.com/findings/5664-new-zealand-roy-morgan-poll-july-2-2014-201407020558|url-status=dead}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web|title=Digipoll: Minor parties surge as Labour sinks lower|url=https://www.nzherald.co.nz/nz/digipoll-minor-parties-surge-as-labour-sinks-lower/X27NS52MJYAS2V45NMFNI7XRCQ/|access-date=31 January 2021|website=[[The New Zealand Herald]] |date=28 August 2014 |language=en-NZ}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web|title=Election 2014: Political dirt doesn't stick to Nats in new Herald-Digipoll|url=https://www.nzherald.co.nz/nz/election-2014-political-dirt-doesnt-stick-to-nats-in-new-herald-digipoll/SRA6J5A5G3SRLTEGXALKOXA2EY/|access-date=31 January 2021|website=[[The New Zealand Herald]] |date=4 September 2014 |language=en-NZ}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web|title=National unscathed by Dirty Politics - poll|url=http://tvnz.co.nz/content/6073705.xml|access-date=31 January 2021|website=TVNZ}}</ref> which would give Internet Mana multiple MPs if it could retain Te Tai Tokerau. The only poll of that electorate suggested a very close race; in that poll 38% said they would give Harawira their electoral vote, while 37% said they would vote for his main challenger, [[New Zealand Labour Party|Labour's]] [[Kelvin Davis (politician)|Kelvin Davis]].<ref>{{Cite web|title=Te Tai Tokerau poll results highlight voter concerns|url=https://www.teaomaori.news/te-tai-tokerau-poll-results-highlight-voter-concerns|access-date=31 January 2021|website=Māori Television|language=en}}</ref>

On election night, Harawira lost Te Tai Tokerau, receiving 8,969 electorate votes to Davis's 9,712.<ref>{{Cite web|last=|first=|date=|title=Official Count Results -- Te Tai Tokerau|url=https://www.electionresults.govt.nz/electionresults_2014/electorate-69.html|access-date=|website=}}</ref> Internet Mana received 1.42% of the party votes.<ref name="2014 general election results2" /> Without an electorate seat or 5% of the party vote, the coalition failed to have any representation.

Dotcom said to reporters on election night that "I take full responsibility for this loss tonight, because the brand—the brand Kim Dotcom—was poison for what we were trying to achieve."<ref name="Poison2">{{cite news|last1=Hutchison|first1=Jonathan|date=20 September 2014|title=New Zealand's Ruling National Party Is Re-elected|work=New York Times|url=https://www.nytimes.com/2014/09/21/world/asia/new-zealands-ruling-national-party-is-re-elected.html|access-date=7 October 2017}}</ref> The parties later separated. After the election, Laila Harré resigned as leader of the Internet Party<ref>{{cite web|date=15 December 2014|title=Harré has officially resigned from the Internet Party|url=http://yournz.org/2014/12/15/harre-has-officially-resigned-from-the-internet-party/|access-date=2 April 2017|publisher=Your NZ}}</ref> and the party told its members that it was concentrating on efforts to build its internal structures to support its grassroots movement.<ref name="Executive Committee By-election2">[https://internet.org.nz/news/95 Executive Committee By-election called], {{Webarchive|url=https://web.archive.org/web/20150204055547/https://internet.org.nz/news/95|date=4 February 2015}} ''Internet Party'', 1 February 2015</ref> In December 2016, [[Kim Dotcom]] posted a poll on [[Twitter]] asking if his followers wanted the Internet Party to stand in the [[2017 New Zealand general election|2017 election]].<ref>{{cite web|title=Kim Dotcom hints at return of the Internet Party|url=http://www.radionz.co.nz/news/political/321259/internet-party-2-point-0|access-date=5 January 2017|website=[[Radio New Zealand]] |date=26 December 2016}}</ref>

===2017 election===
The Internet Party remained leaderless until 8 February 2017, when it appointed [[Suzie Dawson]], an activist and citizen journalist who had been seeking temporary asylum in Russia since 2016, as the party's new leader for the 2017 election.<ref>{{cite news|last1=Hurley|first1=Sam|date=17 June 2017|title=Kim Dotcom's Internet Party names exiled citizen journalist Suzie Dawson as leader|publisher=[[New Zealand Herald]] |url=http://www.nzherald.co.nz/nz/news/article.cfm?c_id=1&objectid=11877476|access-date=7 October 2017}}</ref> The party ran eight list candidates.<ref name="PartyLists20172">{{cite web|date=31 August 2017|title=2017 General Election Party Lists|url=http://www.elections.org.nz/events/2017-general-election/2017-general-election-party-lists|url-status=dead|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20180420193402/http://www.elections.org.nz/events/2017-general-election/2017-general-election-party-lists|archive-date=20 April 2018|access-date=3 September 2017|publisher=Electoral Commission}}</ref> During the [[2017 New Zealand general election|2017 general election]], the Internet Party won 499 votes (0.02%) – the lowest party vote result for any registered party – and failed to win any seats in the [[New Zealand House of Representatives]].<ref name="2017 general election results2" />

In a tweet of April 2018, Suzie Dawson said that she was no longer eligible to run for Parliament as she had lived outside New Zealand for more than three years, and so was resigning as party leader. She said that she had been elected party president instead.<ref name="dawsontweet2">{{Cite tweet|number=989784553601601536|user=Suzi3D|title=Notice: I am no longer eligible to stand as a candidate for Parliament as I am now in excess of 3yrs outside of NZ. Accordingly I have today stepped down as Party Leader of @InternetPartyNZ & have been unanimously appointed to the role of Party President. Full statement to follow|author=Suzie Dawson|date=27 April 2018|access-date=9 January 2023}}</ref>

The party was deregistered on 12 June 2018 because its membership had dropped below the 500 required for registration.<ref name="CANCELLATION OF PARTY REGISTRATION2" />

=== Current status ===
As of August 2022, the Internet Party has not announced that it has dissolved. However, the party has not contested local or general elections since the 2017 general election. The party remains unregistered.<ref>{{Cite web|title=Register of political parties|url=https://elections.nz/democracy-in-nz/political-parties-in-new-zealand/register-of-political-parties/|access-date=8 December 2020|website=elections.nz|language=en-NZ}}</ref> The party applied for broadcasting funding in May 2020 for the [[2020 New Zealand general election|2020 general election]]. It received $41,457 of funding conditional on achieving party registration,<ref>{{Cite web|title=2020 Broadcasting Allocation Decision|url=https://elections.nz/assets/2020-general-election/Broadcasting-Allocation-Decision-2020-29-May-2020-corrected.pdf|website=Elections.nz}}</ref> but it did not register. In August 2022, party founder Kim Dotcom tweeted that the party would be "in the [[Beehive (New Zealand)|Beehive]]" in 2024.<ref>{{Cite web |last=Sowman-Lund |first=Stewart |date=31 August 2022 |title=Kim Dotcom claims Internet Party will be 'in the Beehive' in 2024 |url=https://thespinoff.co.nz/live-updates/31-08-2022/kim-dotcom-claims-internet-party-will-be-in-the-beehive-in-2024 |access-date=31 August 2022 |website=[[The Spinoff]] }}</ref>

== Policies ==
The party used a consultative process to form policies with its membership using online platforms with policy writers following the forum in the background.<ref>{{cite web|title=Internet Party|url=https://internet.org.nz/policies|url-status=dead|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20150113200001/https://internet.org.nz/policies|archive-date=13 January 2015|access-date=23 February 2015}}</ref>

During its launch in 2014, the party set an agenda which included the following broad aims:<ref name="nzh2" /><ref>{{cite web|title=Internet Party Action Agenda|url=https://internet.org.nz/mission|url-status=dead|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20140327220944/https://internet.org.nz/mission|archive-date=27 March 2014|access-date=27 March 2014|publisher=Internet Party}}</ref>

* Provide unlimited high-speed internet to all New Zealanders, 50% cheaper than current prices;
* Build another [[submarine communications cable]] connecting New Zealand to the world;
* Create new high-tech jobs;
* Restrict government surveillance;
* Review the [[Trans-Pacific Partnership]] Agreement;
* Copyright reform;
* Encourage clean energy and green technology;
* Reduce social inequality.

